  - [1mDenylist / scrub[0m — log what's passed, strip known-sensitive keys ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m) on the way out. Less
    friction day to day. Fails open: a field nobody thought to add to the list ships in the clear, and you find out from
    a log dump.
  - [1mDeveloper discipline[0m — no enforcement, just a convention. Cheapest now, and the failure mode is credentials in a log
    store you then have to purge.

  This one is genuinely expensive to change later, and not because of the code. Once records with a password or an
  unhashed identifier land in your log store, you own a cleanup and possibly a disclosure — you can't retract them by
  changing the logger afterward.

  There's a sub-question riding along: [1musernames are PII.[0m They're also the single most useful key for tracing one user's
  failed login. The middle path is logging a stable hash or a per-session correlation ID instead of the raw username —
  you can still follow one session end to end, without the log store becoming an account list.

  [1mMy recommendation: allowlist, plus a correlation ID instead of the raw username, and the password value never enters a
[0m  [1mrecord under any key.[0m The friction is small at this size, and it's the only option where a mistake in a future call
  site is contained by default.

